Short answer: yes — whenever an SSN is used to verify your identity, the name on the document should match the name on file with the Social Security Administration (SSA). Exact matches avoid verification delays or rejections.

More details and what to do
- Why it matters: organizations (employers, banks, government agencies, the IRS, etc.) usually verify SSN + name against SSA records. If the name you enter differs from SSA’s record (different spelling, missing/extra middle name or initial, old name after marriage/divorce), the verification can fail or be flagged for manual review.
- Minor differences: small formatting differences (hyphens, punctuation) are often tolerated. But spelling errors, transposed letters, or using a nickname instead of your legal name can cause problems.
- If your name has changed: update your name with the SSA before relying on SSN-based verifications. To change your name you typically submit Form SS-5 plus original supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport/driver’s license). Processing can take several days to a few weeks depending on the office and method.
- If the form is already submitted and the verification fails: contact the requester and either
  - ask what documentation they will accept (e.g., copy of updated SSN card, marriage certificate, passport, driver’s license), or
  - correct the form to match your SSA record.
- If you’re unsure: check your name as listed by the SSA (my Social Security account or local SSA office) and use that spelling on forms requiring your SSN.

Security note: avoid sharing your full SSN in messages or unsecured channels. If you must provide an SSN on a form, confirm the site or recipient is secure and legitimate; when communicating with people, give only the last four digits unless the full number is absolutely required and you’re using a secure channel.

