Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — the name on the form should match your legal name as it appears in Social Security Administration (SSA) records. A mismatch can cause verification failures, processing delays, or tax/employment reporting problems.

Details and what to do
- Why exactness matters: many organizations (employers, government agencies, banks) verify the name + SSN against SSA records. Differences in spelling, order, or missing parts (middle name vs. initial) can trigger a “name/SSN mismatch.”
- What counts as a mismatch: legal-name differences (e.g., maiden vs. married name, missing or extra middle name, different punctuation or abbreviations) are more likely to cause problems than small formatting differences. But even small typos can fail automated checks.
- Common exceptions: some private organizations may accept minor variations if you provide a supporting photo ID or legal document, but official tax and benefits reporting generally requires the SSA record to match.
- If your name doesn't match SSA records:
  - Update your SSA record to your current legal name (if it’s supposed to be different). You can do this via your local SSA office or online (if eligible) with proof documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate) and form SS-5.
  - Or ask the requesting organization what proof they will accept (driver’s license, passport, marriage certificate, court order). They may accept those temporarily while you update SSA.
- For employers and taxes: make sure the name on Form W-4/W-2 matches SSA records to avoid IRS mismatch notices.
- Security note: I won’t repeat or store your full SSN here. Avoid sending your full SSN in unsecured messages or emails. Only provide it through secure forms (HTTPS) or directly to verified organizations that need it.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether an exact SSA-name match is required and what documents they accept, or
- Give steps and a checklist for updating your name with SSA.

Which would you prefer? Also, I noticed the full SSN and the last-four you wrote don’t match — I won’t repeat the full number, but let me know if you meant a different last four and I’ll tailor the advice.

