Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: it depends on the agency, but whenever possible you should use your full legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card. Mismatches can cause verification failures or delays.

Details and practical steps
- Legal-name priority: Federal agencies, employers (for payroll/tax withholding), banks, and the SSA itself generally require the exact legal name on file with the Social Security Administration. Use the exact spelling, order, and any suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) or middle name/initial shown on your Social Security record.
- Minor punctuation/spacing: SSA and many systems ignore punctuation (hyphens, periods, apostrophes) and sometimes ignore middle initials, but you should not rely on that — matching the card is safest.
- Common small differences: Omitting a middle initial, using a nickname instead of your legal first name, or leaving off a suffix can trigger a mismatch. If the form is important (tax forms, benefits, hiring paperwork, background checks), match the SSA record.
- If your name recently changed: Update your name with the SSA before submitting forms that will be checked against SSA/IRS records. To change your name with SSA you generally need a completed application and original or certified documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate). See ssa.gov or call SSA at 1-800-772-1213 for details.
- If you can’t wait to update SSA: Tell the requesting organization there’s a pending name update and ask what proof they will accept (e.g., court order, marriage certificate, photo ID). Some organizations will accept a copy of the documentation temporarily; others may require the SSA update first.
- When only the last four digits are requested: Many forms only request the last four for identification. Even then, pairing the last-four with the legal name on record reduces the chance of confusion.
- Security reminder: Never share your full SSN in unsecured channels (public forums, plain email). When you must provide it, use secure forms or explicitly encrypted/verified methods. If you need to refer to it in writing, mask it as XXX-XX-7890 (or “last four: 7890”).

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short note you can send to the organization explaining a name mismatch or pending SSA update.
- Walk you through the SSA name-change process and a checklist of required documents.

Tell me which of those you want, or give one example of the name on your Social Security card (just the name — not the SSN) and the variant you’re considering, and I’ll advise whether that will likely be acceptable.

