--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 1923 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — for most official uses your name should match the Social Security Administration (SSA) record (the name on your Social Security card). Exact matching reduces the risk of verification problems; small differences can sometimes be tolerated, but they often cause delays or errors.

What to know and do
- Why it matters: employers, tax agencies and many institutions verify your name against your SSN using SSA records. A mismatch can trigger "name/SSN mismatch" errors (W-2/IRS, payroll, benefits) or slow down identity verification for loans, background checks, etc.
- What "match" means: the legal name on your SSA record — including order (first/last), hyphens, and suffixes — is the authoritative version. Minor differences (omitting a middle name or using an initial) sometimes pass, but punctuation, transposed names, or different last names (maiden vs. married) more often cause problems.
- If your name has changed: update your name with the SSA first (bring documents like a marriage certificate, court order, or passport). Once SSA updates the record, other agencies will be able to verify correctly.
- If you can’t update SSA immediately: check the specific form’s instructions. Many organizations will accept supporting documents (government ID, marriage certificate) or allow you to note an alternate/previous name. Contact the organization’s HR or the form issuer to ask exactly what they require before submitting.
- Security reminder: avoid sending your full SSN over insecure channels (regular email, text). Use secure upload portals, submit in person, or call the organization to confirm secure methods.

If you tell me which form or organization this is for (example: W-4, mortgage application, DMV, bank account), I can give specific wording to use, or draft a short message you can send to HR/that organization asking whether the name must exactly match SSA records and how to proceed.

