Short answer: yes — in most cases your name should match the legal name on file with the Social Security Administration (your Social Security card) or the agency that will verify your SSN. Small differences can sometimes be tolerated, but mismatches are a common cause of delays or rejections.

Details and practical steps
- Why exact matching matters: organizations that check an SSN will typically match the number against the name on SSA records (or their own records). If the spelling or order of names differs, the verification can fail and you may be asked to provide additional documentation.
- What “exact” means in practice:
  - Use your legal first and last name exactly as on your Social Security card (including suffixes like Jr./Sr. if present).
  - Middle names/initials: omission or use of only an initial is often accepted, but matching SSA records is safest.
  - Hyphens, apostrophes, diacritics: some systems ignore punctuation or accents, but some do not. Try to match how it appears in official records.
  - Capitalization usually doesn’t matter; spelling and order of names do.
- If your name changed (marriage, court order, etc.): update your name with SSA first if you’ll be using the SSN under your new name. If you can’t update immediately, be prepared to show legal docs (marriage certificate, court order) to the requester.
- When only last four digits are requested: many forms ask only the last four, but they still expect the name to match the record the requester uses for verification. Don’t assume last-four-only means the name can be different.
- If you’re unsure: check the form’s instructions or contact the requesting organization (employer, bank, government office). They can tell you how strict they are and what supporting documents they require.

Security note
- Avoid sending your full SSN over unsecured email or messaging. Use secure upload portals, in-person delivery, or encrypted channels whenever possible.

Would you like help?
- I can draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether they need the exact SSA name or what documents they’ll accept.
- Or I can walk you through how to check or update your name with the SSA if that’s needed.

