Short answer: yes — for official uses your name should match the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card) whenever possible.

Why it matters
- Agencies that verify identity (Social Security Administration, IRS, employers for payroll/W‑2, benefits offices, background‑check vendors) match your name and SSN. A mismatch can cause delays, rejected forms, processing errors, or tax/benefit notices.
- Small differences (middle initial vs full middle name, inclusion of a suffix like Jr./III, or using a maiden vs married name) are common causes of mismatches.

What to do
1. Check the exact name on your Social Security record:
   - If you have your Social Security card, use the name shown there.
   - You can also check your record at ssa.gov (My Social Security) or by contacting your local SSA office.

2. If the name on the form does not match your SSA record:
   - If the SSA record is correct and the form is wrong, ask the requester (employer, agency) whether you should update the form to match your SSA name.
   - If the SSA record is wrong or out of date (e.g., you changed your name), you should update your Social Security record. To change a name with SSA you generally need proof of identity and proof of the name change (marriage certificate, court order, divorce decree, or U.S. passport/birth certificate as applicable). SSA can advise next steps and whether you must visit an office.

3. Short‑term/workaround:
   - Tell the requester you use a different name and ask whether they can accept documentation showing the legal name or add an “also known as (a.k.a.)” notation. Different organizations have different policies, so check with them directly.

Security note
- Avoid sending your full SSN over insecure channels (email, chat). If an organization needs it, confirm their secure submission method. If you must reference it, provide only the last four digits unless the requester specifically requires the full number and uses a secure transmission method.

If you want, I can:
- Help draft a short message to the agency or HR asking whether the name should exactly match SSA records and explaining the situation.
- Provide the SSA contact/next steps for updating your record (if you tell me which country — I assume U.S. — or confirm you want that).

