Short answer: yes — your name on the form should match the legal name the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on file (the name associated with your SSN). That’s what most agencies, employers, and tax/verification systems compare against.

Details and practical tips:
- Exact match is best. Use the same first, middle, last name and suffix (Jr./Sr./III) as on your Social Security card or your SSA online account. Mismatches can delay processing, trigger identity/verification flags, or cause E‑Verify/IRS problems.
- Small formatting differences are usually tolerated (capitalization, extra spaces, punctuation, hyphens), but actual misspellings or different last names (maiden vs. married) are likely to cause issues.
- Middle name vs. middle initial: often okay, but if the SSA record includes a full middle name, using the full name reduces the chance of a mismatch.
- If you’ve legally changed your name but haven’t updated SSA, update SSA first (they must have your legal name on file for things like wages and benefits to be recorded correctly).
- If the form allows an “also known as,” “maiden name,” or “previous name” field, add the alternate name there and attach supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order) if requested.
- If you already submitted a form with a different spelling, contact the requester and tell them you need to correct the name; they’ll tell you their preferred method (secure portal, corrected form, or documentation).
- Security note: avoid sending full SSNs by unsecured email. Share only the last four digits when possible and use secure portals or encrypted channels when full SSN transmission is required.

If you tell me what type of form this is (employer onboarding, government benefit, tax form, bank, loan, etc.), I can give more specific steps or help draft a short correction/explanation you can send.

