Short answer: yes — in most cases the name on the form should match your legal name as it appears on your Social Security record (your Social Security card). Using the exact legal spelling (including suffixes, hyphens, and whether a middle name or initial is present) avoids verification and tax-reporting problems.

Details and practical tips
- Why it matters: Employers, the IRS, banks, and government agencies match the name you give to your SSN against SSA records. A mismatch can delay processing, cause payroll/tax reporting errors (e.g., W-2 mismatches), or trigger identity-verification requests.
- What “match” means in practice:
  - Exact legal last name and first name are important.
  - Middle name versus middle initial: some systems accept either, but other systems (or the SSA/IRS) may treat them differently — better to match what’s on your Social Security card.
  - Suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) should be included if they appear on your SSA record.
  - Punctuation and diacritics (accents): many electronic systems ignore punctuation/accents, but you should still use the spelling on your SSA record when possible.
- If your name has changed (marriage, court order, etc.): make sure SSA has your updated name before submitting forms that use your SSN. Otherwise update your SSA record first.
- If you’re unsure: ask the organization requesting the SSN whether they require the “legal name as shown on your Social Security card” — that’s a common, clear requirement.
- If there’s already a mismatch or you get an error: the organization will usually tell you what proof they need (e.g., copy of your Social Security card, driver’s license, marriage certificate, court order).
- Security note: because SSNs are very sensitive, avoid posting them in unsecured channels. Use secure portals, in-person delivery, or verified phone numbers when transmitting your SSN.

