Short answer: yes — use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security records (your Social Security card) whenever a form asks for your SSN. Mismatches commonly cause verification problems and delays.

Details and practical steps
- Why it matters: agencies (SSA, IRS, employers, banks) match the SSN to the name on file. If the name you give doesn't match SSA records, the form or transaction can be delayed, rejected, or trigger extra identity checks.
- What "must match" means:
  - First and last name must match the SSA record.
  - Include the same middle name or middle initial and the same suffix (Jr., Sr., III) if those appear on your card.
  - Hyphens, apostrophes, and punctuation are sometimes tolerated, but don’t rely on that — use the same spelling/format as on your Social Security card.
  - Married/changed names: if your name changed but SSA hasn’t been updated, you should either use the old (SSA) name or update SSA first.
- Common consequences of a mismatch: delays in payroll/tax reporting (W-2/1099), rejected benefit claims, extra documentation requests, or problems opening accounts.

What you can do now
1. Check the exact name on your Social Security card or your "my Social Security" online account.
2. If the name on the form doesn’t match SSA records, ask the organization whether they will accept additional ID (driver’s license, passport) or require you to update SSA first.
3. If you need to change SSA records, gather supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate) and follow SSA’s name-change process.
4. If the form allows attaching documents, you can include a copy of your Social Security card and a photo ID (only via whatever secure method the organization requests).
5. If you’re filling the form for taxes, employment, or benefits, prioritize matching SSA records to avoid IRS/employer reporting problems.

A quick note about privacy: I noticed you typed an SSN here. It’s safest not to share full SSNs in chat. I won’t store that information. If you want, tell me what type of form this is (employer, bank, government benefit, tax form) and I can suggest the exact wording to use or help draft a message to the requester asking whether small spelling differences are acceptable.

