Short answer: usually yes — the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record (your legal name) for verification. Small differences can cause delays or mismatches.

More detail and practical steps
- Why exact match matters: employers, the SSA/IRS, banks, background-check companies and benefits agencies use the SSN together with the legal name on file. If the name you give doesn’t match SSA records exactly, the organization may be unable to verify your identity, which can delay processing or cause corrected tax/benefit reporting.
- Minor variations: some places tolerate small differences (e.g., including or leaving out a middle initial), but others do not. Hyphens, punctuation, use of full middle name vs. initial, or using a nickname instead of your legal first name are more likely to cause problems.
- If your name has changed: update your name with the Social Security Administration first (or as soon as possible). If you can’t update before submitting the form, tell the requesting organization you have a name-change in process and provide supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, or current government ID) if they accept them.
- What to do now:
  1. Check the exact name shown on your Social Security card or SSA record (my Social Security online if you have an account).  
  2. Use that exact name on forms that request SSN or explicitly ask for your “legal name.”  
  3. If the form allows an “other names” or “aka” field, list any previous or alternate names there.  
  4. If you expect a mismatch, contact the organization (HR, bank, agency) before submitting and ask whether they require the exact SSA name or will accept supporting documents.
- Security note: avoid sending full SSNs over unsecured email or messages. If someone only needs to identify you, share only the last four digits through a secure channel if requested.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the organization asking whether small spelling differences are acceptable, or
- Walk you through how to check your SSA record or start a name-change with the SSA.

(Also — I noticed you included SSN digits in your message; for your safety, it’s best not to share full SSNs in chat or email.)

