I won’t repeat your SSN here, but I can help — short answer: usually yes, use the exact legal name that’s on your Social Security record; mismatches can cause delays or verification failures. Longer explanation and practical steps:

- Why exact matching matters
  - When an SSN is used to verify identity (tax forms, employment I-9, benefits, background checks, bank accounts, government forms), organizations typically compare the name you provide to the name on file with Social Security Administration (SSA). Differences (order, missing middle name/initial, hyphens, suffixes, married vs. maiden name, small typos) can trigger a rejection or hold while they investigate.

- Common acceptable variations
  - Minor formatting differences (e.g., using a middle initial instead of full middle name) are sometimes OK, but there’s no universal rule — it depends on the organization and the verification method they use.

- If your name on the form does not match your SSA record
  - Best option: use the exact name that appears on your Social Security card.
  - If you’ve legally changed your name and haven’t updated SSA yet, consider updating SSA before submitting documents that rely on SSN/name matching.
  - If you can’t update SSA immediately, contact the organization asking what they accept (e.g., supporting documents: marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license). Some will accept documentation; others may require the SSA record match.

- How to update/confirm your SSA record
  - Check your Social Security card or log into your SSA account to see the exact name on file.
  - To change your name with SSA you typically submit Form SS-5 with proof of identity and proof of the name change (marriage certificate, court order, etc.) — you can do this at a local SSA office or via SSA instructions.

- Security note
  - Only provide your full SSN on secure, trusted forms. When communicating by email or chat, limit to last four digits if possible and avoid posting the full SSN.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether the exact SSA name is required and what documents they’ll accept; or
- Draft the wording to put on the form (e.g., legal name vs. preferred name) if the form has an “other names used” or “aka” field.

Which would you like me to help with?

