Short answer: yes — use the exact legal name that the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on file (the name on your Social Security card). Small punctuation or formatting differences (a missing period after a middle initial, a hyphen, or an extra space) sometimes don’t cause problems, but any substantive difference (nickname instead of legal first name, omitted/added middle name, different last name, wrong suffix) can lead to delays or rejections.

What can happen if the name doesn't match
- Employer / payroll: Your SSN must match SSA records for tax and wage-reporting. A mismatch can delay hiring, payroll setup, or tax withholding records.
- IRS / tax returns: If the name/SSN pair doesn’t match SSA/IRS records, refunds or processing can be delayed.
- Background checks / banking / benefits: Mismatched names can cause failed identity verification, holds, or requests for more documentation.

What differences are usually acceptable
- Minor punctuation/spacing differences (periods, commas, extra spaces) are often tolerated.
- Use or omission of a middle initial sometimes is okay if SSA recognizes the name variant — but it’s not guaranteed.
- However: nicknames (e.g., “Bob” vs “Robert”), different last names (maiden vs married), different order of names, or missing/incorrect suffixes (Jr., III) are more likely to fail verification.

What to do
1. Check the exact name on your Social Security card. If you have it, use that name on the form.
2. If you don’t have the card or recently changed your name, check your SSA record online (my Social Security) or contact SSA.
3. If the SSA record is wrong or you’ve legally changed your name, update SSA before submitting forms that require an exact match. To change your record you’ll need proof (e.g., marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license) and to submit Form SS-5 or visit a local SSA office.
4. If you already submitted a form and it’s rejected for a name/SSN mismatch, contact the requester (employer, agency) to ask what documentation they need and tell them you’re updating SSA if that’s the case.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a short message to your employer/agency explaining a pending name correction.
- Give the checklist of documents SSA requires to change your name.
- Remind you how to refer to your SSN safely (mask it — e.g., last four only) when sending emails.

(I won’t repeat your full SSN here — always avoid sending the full number by unsecured email. Use only what’s necessary, e.g., last 4 digits, unless absolutely required.)

