Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — for anything that will be matched to your Social Security record or used for legal/financial identity verification, the name on the document should match the name on your Social Security record (your SSN card). Small, common variations sometimes pass (e.g., using or omitting a middle initial, or minor punctuation differences), but mismatches can cause delays, rejected forms, or extra identity checks.

Details and practical steps
- Match the SSA record when SSN will be verified. Employers, the IRS, banks, and many government agencies verify the SSN against Social Security Administration (SSA) records. If the name you enter doesn’t match SSA’s record for that SSN, the verification will often fail.
- Use the exact name on your SSN card for tax & employment forms. For W-2s, I-9s, 1099s and tax returns, use the name on your SSN card (first, middle, last, and suffixes) unless you’ve legally changed your name and updated the SSA record.
- Minor differences may be tolerated, but don’t rely on it. Examples that sometimes pass: including a middle initial vs not, dropping a period in an initial, or minor diacritic differences. Examples that commonly cause problems: different last names (maiden vs married), transposed letters, or a different legal first name versus a nickname.
- If your legal name changed: update SSA first. If you’ve legally changed your name (marriage, divorce, court order) you should update your SSA record and get a new SSN card showing the new name before submitting forms that require an exact match.
- If a form only asks for the last four digits: often less strict, but the accompanying name/ID may still be checked — ask the requester what they require.
- If you already submitted a form and it failed: the requester will usually tell you what needs fixing. They may accept supporting documents (driver’s license, passport, marriage certificate), or ask you to re-submit with the name exactly as SSA has it.
- Protect your SSN: avoid posting the full number in messages or public places. If you must share it, do so only over secure channels or directly to verified organizations.

One important note: you wrote “000-00-9012 (last four: 7890),” which is inconsistent (the last four of 000-00-9012 are 9012). Please avoid sharing your full SSN here — I recommend redacting it when talking in chat.

Would you like help with any of the following?
- Checking what name is on your SSN card (if you tell me you’ve changed your name recently).
- Drafting a message to the agency asking whether they require an exact match.
- A short call-script to the SSA or the organization requesting your SSN.

