Short answer: usually yes — for any official use of your SSN (tax forms, payroll, benefits, government ID checks) the name on the document should match the name the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on file. Mismatches can delay processing or trigger verification requests.

Details and practical steps
- What “match” means:
  - Exact legal name as SSA has it (first, middle, last; suffixes like Jr./III; married or maiden name as recorded).
  - Minor differences in capitalization are not an issue. But differences in punctuation, hyphens, order of names, missing suffixes, or use of a nickname instead of your legal first name can cause problems.
- When it matters:
  - Employment paperwork (I-9/payroll), tax forms (W-2), benefits, loan or immigration documents, and most government forms rely on an exact match.
  - Informal or internal forms may be more forgiving, but check with the organization.
- If your name on the form doesn’t match SSA:
  - Contact the organization asking for the SSN and ask what their matching rules are — they may accept a note explaining the variation or request supporting ID.
  - If SSA’s record is outdated/wrong, update SSA’s records (online account at ssa.gov or in person with required documents such as your SSN card, passport, birth certificate, marriage certificate).
  - Provide supporting documentation to the requester (copy of SSA card, marriage certificate, court order) if asked.
- Protecting your SSN:
  - Don’t share the full SSN in unsecured channels (email, chat, public messages). You included it here — consider removing it from posts and avoid sending full SSNs over email. If a form requires the SSN, use a secure (HTTPS) site or a trusted paper process.
  - When you need to reference it in conversation, use only the last four digits.

If you’d like, I can:
- Help draft a brief note to the organization explaining a name mismatch and offering supporting documents.
- Walk you through checking/updating your name with the SSA (what documents you’ll need).
