Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — in most cases the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record (and your Social Security card) exactly or very closely. Mismatches can cause verification failures, tax/benefit reporting problems, payroll delays, or slower processing.

Key details and practical steps:
- What “match” means: exact legal name as recorded with the Social Security Administration (SSA). That includes given name(s), surname, and any suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) as listed on your SSN record.
- Small differences that usually don’t matter: capitalization, extra punctuation (periods, commas), or minor spacing. But don’t rely on this — some systems are strict.
- Differences that commonly cause problems: different last names (married vs. maiden), missing or extra middle name/initial, different ordering of names, different suffixes, or spelling mistakes. If your SSN record uses a version without diacritics or hyphens, use that exact spelling.
- If you’ve legally changed your name: update your record with the SSA first (or show proof of the name change when you submit forms). Employers and financial institutions often require the SSA record to match for payroll, tax withholding, benefits, or background checks.
- If you’re unsure which version SSA has: check your Social Security card or your SSA account online (my Social Security) or call/visit an SSA office.
- When asked for SSN on a form: provide the name exactly as it appears on the SSA record. If the form will be used for verification (employment, taxes, benefits), attach or be prepared to provide supporting documents (SS card, driver’s license, marriage certificate, court order).
- Security reminder: avoid posting or sending your full SSN in insecure channels. Share only the last four digits when possible and only with trusted, verified recipients.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a short note/email to the HR/person handling the form explaining the name discrepancy and what documents you can provide, or
- Walk you through checking/updating your SSA record or what documents you’ll need to change your name with SSA. Which would you prefer?

