Short answer: For official uses (tax forms, Social Security, payroll, government benefits) your name should match exactly the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). For many other, less formal forms some small variations are tolerated, but mismatches between the form and SSA records can cause delays, rejected filings, or payroll/tax problems.

Practical guidance
- Use the legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card (same spelling, order, hyphens, middle name or initial). Small differences (using or omitting a middle initial, nicknames, or punctuation) can sometimes cause problems.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order), update your name with the Social Security Administration before submitting forms that depend on your SSN. Employers and tax agencies match names to SSA records.
- If you can’t update SSA immediately, look for a field to include a former or “also known as” name, and attach supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, or current government ID) when requested.
- For non-government or informal uses (some bank forms, subscriptions), organizations often accept IDs that don’t exactly match SSA records, but confirm with the organization if you’re unsure.
- For employers and tax forms (W-2, 1099), a mismatch between the name submitted and SSA records can trigger IRS/SSA mismatch notices and may affect your tax reporting.

Steps you can take right now
1. Verify the exact name on your Social Security card (or your online My Social Security account).
2. If it’s different from the name on the document, either update the SSA record (visit SSA.gov or your local SSA office) or contact the requester to ask how they want you to proceed (provide proof, use former name field, etc.).
3. Only transmit full SSNs over secure, trusted channels; mask them when possible (e.g., use last four digits) and avoid sending the full SSN in unencrypted messages.

