Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: For official use, yes — the name on the document should match the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Small differences can sometimes be tolerated by people, but automated identity/SSN checks (IRS, payroll, banks, background checks, e-verify, etc.) often require an exact legal-name match (including suffixes and legal last name) and will fail or trigger follow-up if there’s a mismatch.

What “must match” typically means
- Legal name = the name on your Social Security card and government IDs. That’s the name many agencies use for verification.
- Important details: exact last name, first name, and suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) matter. Hyphens, apostrophes, and spacing can matter for some systems.
- Middle name vs middle initial: often tolerated, but some systems need the exact formatting used in SSA records.
- Nicknames (Bob vs Robert), informal spellings, or omitted last names (e.g., maiden vs married) can cause mismatches.

If you’re not sure which name SSA has
- Check your Social Security card or your SSA online account. Use the same spelling/format on the form.
- If SSA’s record is wrong or you legally changed your name, update SSA (bring proof of identity and name-change documents — e.g., marriage certificate, court order, passport/driver’s license).

If you already submitted a form and there’s a mismatch
- Contact the organization (HR, bank, agency) and tell them there’s a name discrepancy. They may ask for a copy of your Social Security card or other ID to reconcile.
- If needed, correct the name with SSA so future verifications match.

Security note
- Don’t post your full SSN in messages or public forms. Only give the full number to trusted recipients over secure channels; otherwise share just the last four if that’s sufficient.

If you’d like, I can:
- Help you draft a short message to HR/whomever to explain the name discrepancy and attach proof, or
- Walk you through what documents you’ll need to update your name with SSA.

