Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — for most official forms that use your Social Security number, the name should match exactly the name on your Social Security Administration (SSA) record (your legal name). Mismatches can cause delays, rejections, or automated “name/SSN mismatch” flags (for payroll, tax forms, benefits, etc.).

Why it matters
- Agencies and employers verify the SSN against SSA records. If the name you give doesn’t match SSA’s record, the system may reject the entry or require manual review.  
- For tax reporting (W-2s, 1099s) a mismatch can trigger IRS notices or delays in processing.

When small differences are usually tolerated
- Minor formatting (periods, commas), omission or inclusion of a middle initial, or standard abbreviations sometimes pass automated checks.  
- But anything that changes the legal name (misspellings, different last name after marriage/divorce, use of a nickname) often causes problems.

What to do next (recommended steps)
1. Check the name on your SSA record (my Social Security account at ssa.gov) to confirm the exact legal name SSA has on file.  
2. If SSA’s name is wrong or needs updating, correct it with SSA (Form SS-5 or via your local SSA office) before submitting the form if feasible.  
3. If your legal name changed (marriage, court order), either:  
   - Use the legal name currently on your SSA record when filling forms, or  
   - Attach a certified copy of the supporting document (marriage certificate, court order) and/or a government ID and follow the recipient’s instructions.  
4. If you can’t change the SSA record right away, contact the organization requesting the SSN and ask what they require (they may accept supporting documents or allow a note that an SSA update is pending).  
5. Always transmit your SSN only over secure channels and avoid sharing the full SSN in unsecured email or web forms.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether their process requires an exact SSA-name match and what supporting documents they accept; or
- Help find the SSA page and forms you’d need to update your name.

Note: I won’t repeat or store your full SSN here. Would you like help drafting the message to the organization?

