Short answer: for most official uses (SSA, IRS, employers, banks, benefits), your name should match exactly the name on your Social Security record. Mismatches are a common cause of delays or verification failures.

A few important details and next steps:

- I won't repeat your SSN here — please avoid posting the full number in chats or emails. I did notice the two values you gave don't match (000-00-9012 vs. last four 7890). If you need help, just tell me which last-four digits are correct — don't type the full SSN.

- Why exact match matters:
  - Agencies that verify SSNs (Social Security Administration, IRS, payroll systems, many banks) compare the name you give to the name on your SSA record. If they don't match, the SSN/name verification can fail and trigger delays.
  - Minor punctuation or spacing differences (periods, commas, extra spaces) are often tolerated by some systems, but differences in last name, use of nicknames vs. legal name, missing/extra middle names or suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) frequently cause problems.

- What to do now:
  1. Check the name on your Social Security record. If you have a mySocialSecurity account you can view it online; otherwise contact your local SSA office or call SSA.
  2. If the name on the SSA record is wrong or uses an old name, correct it with SSA (form SS‑5 and proof of identity/legal name change such as a certified marriage certificate, court order, passport, or birth certificate). Processing times vary.
  3. If you can’t fix SSA records before submitting the form, contact the organization receiving the form and ask whether they accept:
     - A copy of your Social Security card plus a current photo ID,
     - A supplemental document showing the name change (marriage certificate, court order),
     - An “also known as” or previous-name field on the form.
  4. When filling the form, use the legal name that matches your SSA record whenever possible (including suffixes). If the form allows notes, add “Name per Social Security records” or provide the previous name in an “aka” field if relevant.

- Security tip: Only enter your SSN on secure, trusted sites or on forms submitted in person. Avoid sending SSNs over unsecured email or chat.

If you tell me which type of form (tax return, job paperwork, bank account, government benefit) and whether your name has changed or is spelled differently on other IDs, I can give more specific wording you can use to explain the difference or draft a short note to the organization.

