Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — use the name that exactly matches the Social Security Administration's (SSA) records whenever the form will be used to verify your SSN. Mismatches can cause delays or rejections.

Details and practical steps:
- Why exact match matters: organizations that verify SSNs (SSA, IRS, payroll departments, banks, background-check companies) compare the name you give to the name on file for that SSN. Even small differences (missing/extra middle name or initial, hyphenation, maiden vs. married name, or transposed letters) can trigger a mismatch.
- When small differences may be OK: for some informal forms or internal records slight variations are tolerated. For tax, benefits, employment, banking, or government identity checks you should match SSA exactly.
- If your current legal name differs from SSA’s record:
  - Either provide the name exactly as SSA has it, or
  - Update your name with SSA (so their records reflect your current legal name) before submitting the form.
- How to update SSA: you’ll typically need proof of identity plus legal-name-change documentation (marriage certificate, court order, divorce decree, passport, etc.). You can check or start the process at the SSA website or by contacting your local SSA office.
- If you can’t update SSA right away: contact the organization requesting the form, explain the situation, and ask what they accept — they may accept supporting documents (ID, marriage certificate) or give temporary instructions.
- Security note: don’t send your full SSN over insecure channels (regular email, chat). Use the form’s secure upload or the requester’s secure portal, and only provide the SSN when absolutely necessary.

If you tell me what kind of form it is (tax, employment, bank, benefits, etc.) I can give more specific guidance and a short template you can send to the requesting organization.

