Short answer: yes — whenever a form will be verified against your Social Security record (employment, tax forms, benefits, background checks, bank ID checks), the name you enter should match exactly the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Mismatches often cause verification failures or processing delays.

Details and practical tips
- What “match exactly” means: use the same legal name, including the same spelling, hyphens, suffixes (Jr., III), and the same order (first, middle, last) as on your SSA record. If your SSA record includes a middle name, using only an initial can sometimes be accepted, but exact matches are safest.
- Common problem areas:
  - Married/maiden names: Use whichever name is on your Social Security card. If you changed your name but didn’t update SSA, use the old name on the card or update SSA first.
  - Hyphens, apostrophes, and punctuation: Some systems strip punctuation, but some require it. If possible, match SSA exactly.
  - Middle names/initials: Employers and the IRS sometimes accept either, but exact match avoids rejects.
- Consequences of mismatches: delays in processing, rejected tax filings or W-2 reporting, failures in identity verification, and extra paperwork to correct records.
- If the form is not being verified against SSA (a casual form or internal survey), exact matching is less critical — but don’t assume. When in doubt, ask the form requester whether the name must match SSA.

If your name doesn’t match SSA
1. Contact the form requester and ask if they can accept an explanation or attach supporting documentation (marriage certificate, court order, passport).
2. If you need SSA to reflect a new name, update your record with the Social Security Administration (SSA) — you’ll need proof of identity and proof of the name change (e.g., marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate).
3. Keep copies of any communications and documentation in case you need to show proof later.

Security note
I noticed you included a full SSN plus “last four.” For your safety, avoid posting your full SSN in chats. If you want, share only the last four digits or say “last four are XXXX” when asking questions.

Would you like help with any of the following?
- Drafting a message to the organization asking whether they require an exact SSA match.
- Finding what documents you’d need to update your name with the SSA.
- Drafting the correction if you want to request a name change on the form.

