Short answer: usually yes — for anything that will be checked against Social Security records (tax returns, employment verification, benefits), you should use the exact name that appears on your Social Security record. Small differences can cause delays or verification failures.

Details and practical steps:
- Why it matters: agencies and systems (SSA, IRS, E-Verify, many employers and banks) compare the name you give with the name on file for that SSN. A mismatch — different spelling, missing/extra middle name or initial, or a changed last name — can lead to a rejected verification, delayed processing, or requests for additional documentation.
- Minor variations: sometimes punctuation, spacing, or an omitted middle initial won’t break a match, but you can’t count on that. Best practice is to match exactly.
- If you changed your name: update your Social Security record before submitting forms that will be verified. To change the name on your SSN you’ll need to file with the SSA (Form SS-5) and provide proof of identity and legal name-change documents (marriage certificate, court order, etc.).
- If you can’t update immediately: on the form look for fields like “other names used” or attach a copy of the legal name-change document. If this is an employer form (I-9/E-Verify), tell HR — they can advise how to proceed and what documentation to provide.
- For tax filing: the name on your tax return should match SSA/TIN records to avoid processing delays or refund holds.
- Security reminder: don’t share your full SSN over insecure channels (public forums, unencrypted email). If you need to send it, use a secure method and only to trusted parties.

If you tell me which specific form or agency (e.g., I-9 for a new job, a bank account application, tax return, benefits form), I can give exact steps or draft a short note you can attach explaining any name discrepancy.

