Short answer
- For official uses tied to your Social Security Number (tax forms, payroll, Social Security benefits, government IDs), your name should match the legal name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Mismatches can cause delays or errors.
- For some non-government forms, small formatting differences (periods, spaces, using a middle initial vs full middle name) are often accepted, but it depends on the recipient’s rules and their verification system.

Details and common cases
- Tax/employment (W-2, payroll, employer verification): Use the exact legal name on your SSA record so the IRS and SSA can match your SSN to you.
- Government benefits / Social Security: Must match SSA records.
- Banks, lenders, background checks: They usually need official-name matching; small punctuation differences are sometimes tolerated but can still trigger manual review.
- Married / changed names: If you legally changed your name (marriage, court order, etc.), update your SSA record first; otherwise the SSN/name pairing will not match.
- Hyphens, accents, suffixes (Jr., III), middle names/initials, nicknames: These can matter. Systems vary — accents are often dropped, but suffixes and the exact spelling/sequence of names frequently matter.

What to do now (practical steps)
1. Check your Social Security record: sign into your My Social Security account at ssa.gov or contact SSA (1-800-772-1213) to confirm the name on file.
2. Read the form’s instructions or FAQ — it often states whether names must match SSA/ID exactly.
3. If the form requires the SSA/legal name and your document uses a different spelling, either:
   - Update the form to the legal name on your SSN record; or
   - Update your SSA record (bring certified proof of the name change such as a marriage certificate, court order, or passport) before submitting important forms.
4. If you can’t change the SSA record immediately, contact the organization receiving the form and ask whether they will accept the current spelling and what documentation would resolve a mismatch.
5. Keep copies of any supporting documents (name-change certificate, passport, driver’s license) to submit if asked.

