Short answer: yes — use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security card whenever possible. Mismatches (even small ones) can cause identity-verification failures, processing delays, or incorrect records.

Details and practical steps
- Why it matters: Agencies and employers verify the name you provide against Social Security Administration (SSA) records when they match SSNs. If the spelling or format differs from the SSA record, the SSN/name pair may not validate and you may be asked to correct it or provide extra documentation.
- What “exact” means: The SSA’s record is the source of truth. Use the same first/middle/last name, punctuation/hyphens, and order as on your Social Security card. Minor differences sometimes accepted (e.g., using a middle initial instead of a full middle name), but there’s no guarantee — best to match exactly.
- If your name has legally changed: Update your SSA record first (SSA.gov or local SSA office) and get a corrected Social Security card or documentation. Typical proof includes marriage certificate, court order, or birth certificate.
- If you’ve already submitted a form with a misspelling: Contact the requester (HR, agency, bank) right away, explain the issue, and ask whether they need you to resubmit or provide supporting documents. They can tell you their preferred correction process.
- If the form only requests the last four digits: Even when only the last four are requested, make sure your name matches the SSA record or the name on the account — it still helps prevent confusion or mismatches.
- Security reminder: Avoid sharing your full SSN over insecure channels (unencrypted email, public forms). Use secure portals, in-person submission, or mailed forms to trusted addresses.

Would you like me to
- check wording you plan to use on the form to match SSA style, or
- draft a short message to HR/agency explaining a name-correction and asking what they need?

(Note: I won’t store or repeat your full SSN — if you want help referencing it in a message, tell me whether to use “last four digits” only.)

