Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — for any form that’s going to be matched against your Social Security record or used for tax, employment, benefits, or identity verification, you should use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security card (or SSA records). That prevents verification failures, tax/benefit delays, and extra paperwork.

Details and practical guidance:
- Why exactness matters: agencies (SSA, IRS), employers, and some financial institutions compare the name + SSN (and often DOB) against SSA records. A mismatch can cause rejections, delayed processing, or requests for proof of identity.
- What “exact” means in practice: spelling must match. Use the same first, middle (or middle initial) and last name format as on the card. Case (upper/lower) normally doesn’t matter, but punctuation, hyphens, apostrophes, and the exact spelling do.
- Minor acceptable differences: some organizations will accept common abbreviations or dropped middle names (e.g., “Robert J. Smith” vs “Robert Smith”), but this is not guaranteed. Don’t assume—if the form will be verified electronically (IRS, SSA/E-Verify), match SSA exactly.
- If you use a different name in daily life (nickname, married/maiden name): if the form asks for “legal name,” use the legal name. If there’s a field for “also known as” or “previous name,” fill that in. If no such field and the organization accepts documentation, attach or note the other name.
- If your name in SSA records is wrong or outdated: contact the Social Security Administration to correct it. You’ll need proof (birth certificate, passport, marriage certificate, etc.). Fixing SSA records is the correct long-term solution.
- If you’re already filling a form and unsure:  
  - Check your Social Security card or SSA online account for the exact name.  
  - If you can’t access that right now, give the organization the name that matches your primary ID (passport/driver’s license) and ask whether they require the SSA version.  
  - If the form is submitted and flagged, the organization will usually ask for documentation; being proactive avoids rework.

Security note: don’t share your full SSN in insecure channels. When you must provide an SSN in a message or form here, prefer giving only the last four digits (as you already did).

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether they require the exact SSA name and what to do if it differs, or
- Draft the email to SSA to begin a name-correction request.

Which would you like?

