Short answer: usually yes — the name on the form should match the legal name associated with your Social Security number (the name on your Social Security record). If the names don’t match exactly, you can run into verification problems, tax/benefit delays, or employment paperwork issues.

Details and practical steps
- Why an exact match matters
  - The SSA, IRS, employers, banks, and many government forms verify the SSN against SSA records. If the name and SSN don’t match, automated checks can fail and processing can be delayed.
  - “Exact” means your legal name as the SSA has it (including use of a middle name vs. middle initial, hyphens, etc.). Nicknames or informal spellings can cause mismatches.

- What counts as a minor difference
  - Small formatting differences (periods, commas) sometimes pass automated checks, but differences in spelling, missing/extra middle names, or a different last name usually cause problems.
  - If you routinely use a version of your name (e.g., with a maiden vs. married name), it’s safest to use the legal name on your SSA record.

- If the name on the form doesn’t match
  - Ask the requester whether they accept the alternate spelling or need official proof. Many employers/agents will accept supporting documents temporarily (e.g., SSN card, proof of name change).
  - Update your SSA record if your legal name has changed or is recorded incorrectly. To change or correct your name with SSA you’ll typically submit Form SS‑5 and original or certified documents (birth certificate, marriage certificate, court order, passport, and ID). See the Social Security Administration website or local office for exact requirements.
  - For tax issues, the IRS may delay refunds if name/SSN mismatch; resolving it with SSA first is advisable.

- If you can’t update the SSA record right away
  - Provide the SSN card or other acceptable ID to the requester and explain you’re in the process of correcting SSA records.
  - Keep copies of supporting documents showing the name change (marriage certificate, court order).

Privacy note
- Please avoid posting your full SSN in chats or public places. I also noticed the digits you posted don’t match (you wrote 000-00-9012 and then “last four: 7890”). If you need help based on the last four only, you don’t need to share more here.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the form requester or HR asking whether they require the exact SSA-record name and what documents they’ll accept; or
- Walk you through how to start correcting your SSA record (what documents you’ll need). Which would you prefer?

